  "description": "<div><p>Cut string s into as many consecutive, nonempty pieces as possible so that every distinct character appears in only one piece. Return the lengths of the pieces in their original left-to-right order.</p><p>If s is empty, return an empty array. The maximum-number-of-pieces rule fixes a single sequence of lengths.</p><p><strong>Example 1</strong></p><pre>Input: s = \"ababcbacadefegdehijhklij\"\nOutput: [9,7,8]</pre><p>Explanation: The first nine characters share letters only with each other, followed by pieces of lengths seven and eight.</p><p><strong>Example 2</strong></p><pre>Input: s = \"eccbbbbdec\"\nOutput: [10]</pre><p>Explanation: The character e occurs at both ends, forcing everything into one piece.</p><p><strong>Example 3</strong></p><pre>Input: s = \"abc\"\nOutput: [1,1,1]</pre><p>Explanation: Each character occurs only once, so three single-character pieces are best.</p><p><strong>Constraints</strong></p><ul><li>0 ≤ s.length ≤ 10,000.</li><li>s contains only lowercase English letters.</li></ul></div>",
